course: space
lesson: 4
title: Venus
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Venus is almost exactly the size of the Earth and is our nearest planet, so it is called Earth's twin, but it is a very evil twin.
explain: It is wrapped in thick carbon dioxide air under clouds of sulphuric acid, and that blanket traps heat like a car parked in the sun with the windows shut.
explain: So Venus is hotter than Mercury, about 465 degrees everywhere, day and night, and the air presses down as hard as the sea 900 metres deep.
explain: Venus also spins backwards and very slowly: there the Sun rises in the west, and a day lasts longer than a year.
explain: From the Earth it is the brightest thing in the night sky after the Moon, so bright that people sometimes report it as a UFO.
fact: Venus is almost the same size as the Earth and is our nearest planetary neighbour, which is why it is sometimes called Earth's twin.
fact: Venus is the hottest planet, with a surface of about 465 degrees Celsius, day and night, poles and equator alike, because its thick carbon dioxide air traps heat.
fact: The air pressure on Venus's surface is about 90 times the Earth's, like being about 900 metres deep in the ocean.
fact: Soviet Venera landers in the 1970s and 1980s survived only about an hour or two before the heat and pressure destroyed them.
fact: Venus is wrapped in thick clouds of sulphuric acid that hide its surface completely from ordinary telescopes; its surface was mapped by radar, for example by the Magellan probe in the 1990s.
fact: Venus spins backwards (astronomers say retrograde) compared with most planets, so on Venus the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east.
fact: Venus takes about 243 Earth days to spin once but only about 225 days to orbit the Sun, so its day is longer than its year.
fact: After the Moon, Venus is the brightest natural object in the night sky, often called the Morning Star or Evening Star, though it is a planet, not a star.
fact: Venus has more volcanoes than any other planet, and studies of old Magellan radar images, published in 2023 and 2024, found lava flows that changed, so some are probably still active.
fact: Venus's whole surface is quite young, perhaps 300 to 700 million years old, probably covered over by fresh lava, so it has fairly few craters.
fact: Venus may once have had oceans, but it lost its water to space as it heated up; scientists still debate how long any oceans lasted.
fact: NASA's DAVINCI and VERITAS missions and Europe's EnVision have been planned to visit Venus in the 2030s, though their dates and funding keep changing.
exercise: Why is Venus called Earth's twin? | It is almost the same size and is our nearest planet | It has the same oceans and forests | It has the same air to breathe | It has a moon just like ours
exercise: Which is the hottest planet? | Venus | Mercury | Mars | The Earth
exercise: About how hot is the surface of Venus? | About 465 degrees Celsius | About 46 degrees Celsius | About minus 180 degrees Celsius | About 5,500 degrees Celsius
exercise: Is Venus colder at night than in the day? | No, it is about the same day and night | Yes, it freezes hard every single night there | Yes, but it is cold at the poles only, all year | No, it is much hotter at night than by day
exercise: How strong is the air pressure on Venus's surface? | About 90 times the Earth's | About the same as the Earth's | About half the Earth's | There is no air at all
exercise: What are Venus's clouds made of? | Sulphuric acid | Water vapour like ours | Frozen methane | Dust and sand
exercise: How did scientists map the hidden surface of Venus? | With radar, for example from the Magellan probe | By looking down through small gaps in the thick clouds | From photos taken by the astronauts who flew past it | By guessing from its yellow colour and how it shines
exercise: Where does the Sun rise on Venus? | In the west | In the east | In the north | It never rises
exercise: Which is longer on Venus? | The day, about 243 Earth days | The year, about 365 Earth days | Both are 24 hours | Neither, Venus does not spin
exercise: How long does Venus take to orbit the Sun? | About 225 Earth days | About 88 Earth days | About 12 Earth years | About 365 Earth days
exercise: What is the brightest natural object in the night sky after the Moon? | Venus | Mars | The North Star | Jupiter
exercise: What are the Morning Star and the Evening Star? | Venus, seen before sunrise or after sunset | Two different bright stars that rise at different times | Sirius and the North Star, the brightest stars | Comets that come round once every single day
exercise: Which planet has the most volcanoes? | Venus | Mars | The Earth | Mercury
exercise: What did new studies of old Magellan images find? | Lava flows that changed, so some volcanoes are probably active | Rivers of liquid water | Signs of trees on the surface | Footprints of earlier landers
exercise: How old is Venus's surface? | Quite young, perhaps 300 to 700 million years | Exactly as old as the Sun, and never changed since | Only a few thousand years old, made very recently | Older than the Solar System, from an earlier star
exercise: What may have happened to Venus's water? | It was lost to space as Venus heated up | It froze into giant ice caps | It is still in deep oceans under the clouds | It was carried off by the Moon
exercise: Which missions have been planned to visit Venus in the 2030s? | DAVINCI, VERITAS and EnVision | Apollo, Gemini and the Mercury missions | Voyager 1, Voyager 2 and Pioneer 10 | Curiosity, Perseverance and Spirit
test: why's Venus hotter than Mercury when Mercury's way closer?? | Venus's thick carbon dioxide air traps the heat | Venus has a hotter core | Venus is actually closer to the Sun | Venus has volcanoes on fire all the time
test: A car left in the sun with the windows closed gets hot inside. Why is that like Venus? | Heat gets in but cannot easily get out | The car's metal burns like Venus's rocks | The car spins backwards | The car is covered in acid
test: Why did landers on Venus stop working so quickly? | The heat and crushing pressure destroyed them | Acid rain dissolved their parachutes in space | They could not find any light | Volcanoes buried them on landing
test: You stand on Venus (in an impossible super suit). Where do you look for the sunrise? | West | East | North | Straight overhead
test: My dad reported a weird bright light low in the west after sunset as a UFO. What was it probably? | Venus | Mars | A comet | The North Star
test: In a film, astronauts land on Venus, open their helmets and breathe. What is wrong? | Venus's air is thick carbon dioxide under acid clouds and crushingly hot | Nothing, Venus has air like ours | Only the clouds are wrong, the ground is fine | They should have landed at night when it is cool
test: If you had a birthday every Venus year, would you get more or fewer birthdays than days? | More birthdays, because its year is shorter than its day | Fewer birthdays, because its year is much longer | The same number, they are equal | No birthdays, Venus has no year
test: Mia says Venus has always been a dead, unchanging world. What did recent studies suggest? | Some of its volcanoes are probably still active | Venus has rivers of water flowing on it even today | Venus has no volcanoes at all, not even any old ones | Venus is slowly freezing over, from the poles down
test: Why does Venus have fairly few craters? | Its surface is fairly young, renewed perhaps by lava | Nothing ever hits Venus | Its acid clouds melt all the craters | Its moons catch all the rocks
test: What could Venus teach us about the Earth? | How a twin-sized planet could lose its oceans and overheat | That planets cannot have water | That the Earth is getting colder | That all planets spin backwards
test: That Morning Star in the east before dawn — it's a star, yeah? | No, it is the planet Venus | Yes, it is the closest star | Yes, it is the Sun's twin star | No, it is a satellite
exam: State the principal reason for which the surface temperature of Venus exceeds that of Mercury. | Its dense carbon dioxide atmosphere retains heat | It is situated considerably nearer the Sun than Mercury | It possesses a hotter metallic core | It is illuminated continuously on all sides
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the rotation of Venus? | It rotates in the retrograde sense and its day exceeds its year | It rotates once every twenty-four hours in the usual sense | It does not rotate whatsoever | It rotates more rapidly than any other planet
exam: Ascertain the means by which the surface of Venus has been mapped notwithstanding its clouds. | Radar observation from orbiting spacecraft | Photography through openings in the cloud layer | Direct survey by crewed expeditions | Observation by ordinary telescopes from the Earth
exam: Identify the conclusion drawn from studies published in 2023 and 2024 concerning the geology of Venus. | Certain volcanoes are probably presently active | All volcanic activity ceased billions of years ago | Its surface is covered by liquid water | It possesses no volcanoes of any description
